y\n184-read as the same number. Characters are a distinct class only where the\n--\n199-operands. A producer can scan those tags to identify common-domain-safe images.\n200-A receiving host that lacks a class refuses the image with a qualified\n201-`unsupported-value` outcome before execution; this is a host-boundary outcome,\n202-not a stream gap.\n203-\n204:The code-image identity H is computed by one B1 function, `image-hash`, over\n205-the descriptor hash, including its lowering-contract version, and the\n206-canonical positional instruction vector: pc-indexed tuples, refs resolved to\n207-pcs, no header, and exact scalar bytes. Provenance is\n208:a diagnostic side table indexed by pc and is outside the hash. H is the only\n209-executable and sharing identity. No projection fingerprint or second identity\n210-is attached to the image, request, response, verification, or cache. Any later\n211-cache is keyed by H.\n212-No cache is specified by B0 through B5; any later cache is an explicit value\n213-keyed by H. A receiver hashes the received canonical wire bytes before\n214-decoding; those bytes are H's preimage. `jing/segment-key` is only the storage\n215-address for the same bytes and is not H; `yin.vm.content` stores and fetches\n216:bytes, while verification recomputes `image-hash`. H is computed using explicit\n217-SHA-256 and is pinned by the VM format contract version; it is completely\n218-independent of DaoJing's `default-hash-algorithm` and does not change when\n219-DaoJing defaults to BLAKE3.\n220-\n221-## 3. Lowering and scope\n222-\n223-Lowering is two stages, both pure stream-to-stream functions:\n224-\n--\n470-   `:yin/blocked` (both done by the engine). B3's `(peek stack)` is\n471-   equivalent for pure programs and stays as the halting write.\n472-3. Register payload. `{:segment :pc :frames :stack :continuation :format\n473-   :hash}`. `:segment` names the image as the record holds it, in the same\n474-   role as the semantic VM's segment id; while one image is loaded it is\n475:   that image. `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and `:hash` is the loaded\n476:   image's H: every parked record and reified continuation carries the\n477-   model and image identity that produced it, so a continuation is never\n478-   interpreted by a VM or against an image other than its own. These keys\n479-   do not collide with the engine-owned set and the engine never reads\n480-   them. Free environment, store, primitives, and modules are not\n481-   registers and stay on the state map.\n482-4. Restore. One function, `stack-restore [base entry val]`, which first\n483-   refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format` outcome unless the\n484-   entry's `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and its `:hash` equals the\n485-   loaded image's H, then writes the registers from the entry and conjes\n486-   `val` onto the restored `:stack` (the B3 obligation that every\n487-   value-producing opcode lands on the stack, since there is no\n488:   accumulator). The refusal is the same-model, same-image rule the\n489-   register design states in its section 5.1; cross-model transport is\n490-   not a lift here and is deliberately unsupported. The FFI two-step\n491-   (engine design section 4) lives here, on the entry keys `:request-sent`\n492-   and `:call-id`, using `ffi/call-result` and `ffi/response-wait-entry`.\n493-   The walker's frame-typed placement is not used: this machine's\n494-   continuation is a vector of return frames.\n495-5. Builders. Per blocking instruction, a `:stream/put` and `:stream/next`\n496-   builder closing over the post-instruction registers (`pc` advanced,\n--\n548-    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\n549-    Existing edits: none\n550-    Must not change: yin.vm.code, :yin.code/*, merged projection namespace\n551-\n552-Define and export the descriptor, derived opcode table, exact executable\n553:scalar encoder, code-image hash, and validator. Completion includes malformed\n554-rows,\n555-out-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes\n556-for composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either\n557-encoded or refused with `:unsupported-value` (JVM encodes, CLJS and\n558-ClojureDart both refuse), distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0` on JVM and Dart,\n559-distinct hashes for ratios and chars on the JVM, and identical bytes\n560-across hosts for the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H\n561-values for a frozen corpus are checked on all three host lanes. A\n--\n680-    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n681-    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n682-    Existing edits: none\n683-    Depends on: dao.jing (segment-key, materialize!, get), dao.jing.dht\n684-    (create-content-dht, IDhtNet), dao.jing.remote (the DaoStream\n685:    transport and its server handlers), B1 (image-hash, image-defect,\n686-    descriptor), B2 (lift)\n687:    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\n688-    dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, named VM semantics\n689-\n690-B6 is specified in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, the\n691-standalone linker design for Phase B6, used by both the stack and register\n692-VM for linking code over `dao.stream`. That document specifies the format-\n693-parameterized fetch function over `dao.stream` / `dao.jing`, the address and\n694-identity distinction, the six-step fetch protocol, the qualified refusal\n695-vocabulary, the H and R indexes, the transitional content-hash risk, and the\n--\n712-\n713-The matrix includes exact scalar spelling, names, tail flags,\n714-provenance, scope, duplicate parameters, nil fill, extras dropped, control\n715-flow, streams, effects, continuations, macros, malformed images, and all\n716-existing parity/content/completion execution fixtures. It also includes\n717:alpha-equivalent binder renames with equal image hashes, exact-spelling and\n718-tail-flag differences with distinct hashes, free-name changes, stream transfer,\n719-and cross-host code-image bytes.\n720-\n721-Non-goals are equality saturation, a new JIT, global distribution beyond B6\n722-and B7, and any change to the merged dormant projection or its fingerprint.\n723-\n724-### 7.1 Prior art: Unison's runtime\n725-\n--\n782-decision.\n783-\n784-`dao.jing` stores and fetches the image as a value at its own content\n785-address, over `dao.stream` through `dao.jing.remote`. B verifies the\n786-received value twice, against its Jing address with `segment-key` and\n787:against H with `image-hash`, and never treats `jing/segment-key` as H. The\n788-integrity rule is the same as hashing wire bytes before trusting them; the\n789-mechanics are Jing's, which hash the decoded value, and the B6 box states\n790-them. `load-vector` and `:code-aliases` remain named-VM machinery. B6 does\n791-not require persistent publication or discovery: the composition supplies\n792-the Jing handle and the H index.\n793-\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -A8 -B5 'register-hash|:hash R|R is|same-image|image identity|:hash' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | head -n 145\"","aggregated_output":"88-is decided as the resolver-and-stack-lowerer split described in section\n89-2.2 below; everything else in `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` is unchanged.\n90-\n91-### 1.1 Identity and sharing\n92-\n93:The register descriptor is `:yin.debruijn.register/*`. Its `register-hash`\n94-function, R, hashes the register descriptor hash and the canonical\n95-positional register vector, exactly as B1's `image-hash` hashes the stack\n96:descriptor hash and the canonical stack vector. R is the only identity used\n97-to fetch, verify, cache, or execute register bytes. A linker request must\n98-name both the format and R, for example\n99:`{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`.\n100-\n101:R is alpha-invariant for binder renames for the same reason H is: the\n102-register vector carries `:load-bound` depth and position operands and\n103-`:closure` arities, and binder names live in a diagnostic side table outside\n104-the hash. Equal R therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling, free\n105-name operands, and front-end tail flags, as equal H does. Each format is\n106-deterministically derived from the resolved tuples, so equal resolved\n107-tuples give equal H and equal R, and a binder rename changes neither. No\n108:biconditional between H and R is claimed: two deterministic projections may\n109-preserve different distinctions, and the corpus tests are evidence for the\n110-one-directional law only.\n111-\n112-This is not a second semantic answer to alpha-equivalence. A register\n113-lowerer cannot repair differences in exact scalar spelling, free names, or\n114-front-end tail flags; those differences already make the resolved tuples,\n115-and so both H and R, differ.\n116-\n--\n282-\n283-The lowerer's R formula is:\n284-\n285-    R = sha256(register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)\n286-\n287:Both components are canonical bytes. R is an executable-format identity\n288-pinned to explicit SHA-256 by VM contract freeze, completely decoupled from\n289-DaoJing's `default-hash-algorithm`. A receiver verifies R over the\n290-received image before trusting it, as B1's wire rule requires for H; the\n291-descriptor hash and contract version are inside R, so descriptor\n292-agreement is that same check. It then validates, runs the receiver\n293-closure check over `:load-free` operands exactly as D11 and D15 define it\n294-for stack images, and only then executes. No relation to a stack H is\n295-embedded or checked.\n--\n631-### 5.1 Continuation transport across VM models\n632-\n633-A parked continuation resumes only under the VM model, and the image\n634-identity, that parked it. This is a decided rule for B4 and R4, not a\n635-gap. Every parked record and reified continuation of either VM carries\n636:its model and image identity in its register payload, `{:format\n637::yin.debruijn.code :hash H}` or `{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`\n638-beside the fields the engine seam already lists, and a restore whose VM or\n639-image does not match refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format`\n640-outcome instead of interpreting a foreign payload. The engine never reads\n641-those keys; they are register payload under the section 3 rule of\n642-`yin.vm.engine.md`.\n643-\n644-Direct cross-model resume is not possible, and a pairwise lift is the\n645-wrong shape. A register park site and a stack park site for the same\n--\n752-     :live [register-index ...]\n753-     :continuation [<return frame> ...]\n754-     :dest <register-index or nil>\n755-     :resume-mode :write-result | :return-result\n756-     :format :yin.debruijn.register\n757:     :hash <R>}\n758-\n759-`:live` is copied from the instruction's verified operand. `:regs` is a\n760-vector in the same strictly ascending index order and has exactly the same\n761-indices. A saved value is read from the running register file at snapshot\n762-time. Maps are not used because their iteration order is not an encoding\n763-contract. `:dest` is `rd` for `:write-result` and nil for\n764-`:return-result`. The destination is not in `live`; restoration writes it.\n765-\n766-Each non-tail register call pushes a return frame of the same sparse form:\n767-\n768-    {:segment <validated register image>\n769:     :hash <R>\n770-     :site-pc <call pc>\n771-     :return-pc <pc after call>\n772-     :frames <caller lexical frames>\n773-     :regs [[register-index value] ...]\n774-     :live [register-index ...]\n775-     :dest <call rd>}\n776-\n777-The outer payload's `:continuation` is a vector of these frames. An image\n--\n937-   are refused.\n938-4. The pure effect constructor produces the same normalized descriptors as\n939-   the stack B4 path for stream make, put, cursor, next, close, and dynamic\n940-   primitive effects. Store and gensym value/state transitions match too.\n941-5. Sparse snapshots contain exactly the verified live register pairs,\n942:   destination, image identity, lexical frames, and return frames. Tests\n943-   cover current-continuation, explicit park/resume, non-tail and tail\n944-   primitive effects, immediate stream success, blocked put and next, and\n945-   both FFI wait stages.\n946-6. Wait-entry validation refuses missing resource ids, a reason/shape\n947-   mismatch, an out-of-bounds destination, a foreign format or R, an\n948-   ill-formed live set, every stale wake key named in section 5.2.3, and a\n949-   raw host exception or other non-plain resume value.\n950-7. FFI tests require the call pair before parking, preserve request bytes\n--\n1098-  pairing's trust rests on; R4 and B6 are both decided phases.\n1099-\n1100-## 9. End condition\n1101-\n1102-The lowerer-only end condition is a validated, deterministic register image\n1103:whose R is reproducible on all three hosts, whose address law with the stack\n1104-image holds over the parity corpus, and whose named-VM normalized fixtures\n1105-agree. The full register-VM end condition adds a validator-approved kernel\n1106-with both R4 tiers complete, the R3 report published for the owner to read,\n1107-and cross-stream R fetch and verification via the B6 linker. The\n1108-project does not stop at R2: R3's numbers inform, they do not decide.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
